---
title: Client islands
summary: Mark an import with hydrate and the component gets JS on a schedule. Everything else stays static.
related:
  - islands/timing-nesting
  - islands/server-islands
  - islands/lakes
---

A **client island** is a component that becomes interactive. You mark its import with `hydrate`, and ogygia ships JS for that component on a schedule. The rest of the page stays server HTML with zero client JS.

> **When to use.** Reach for a client island whenever a piece of the page needs to respond to the user — a counter, a menu, a form widget, a chart. If it never needs JS, leave it unmarked (it stays HTML) or make it a [lake](/docs/islands/lakes).

```svelte
<script>
  import Counter from './Counter.svelte' with { wake: 'load' };
</script>

<Counter start={3} />
```

The marked import binding **is** the island — a portable wrapper. Use it like any component: `<Counter />`, a dynamic `<Comp />`, or a `{#each}` list of `{ Comp, props }`. See [Portable bindings](/docs/islands/portable-bindings).

## The schedules

`hydrate` takes the same timing words everywhere:

| Value | JS runs |
| --- | --- |
| `load` | right away |
| `idle` | when the browser is idle |
| `visible` | when scrolled into view |
| `interaction` | on the first pointer / key / focus inside it — the waking click replays, typing survives |
| `'(max-width: 600px)'` | when the media query matches |

Same import binding wraps the demos below — each is a real island on this page, marked with a different schedule. Reload with JS disabled and the markup is still here; the widgets just stay static.

<script>
  import LoadLive from './LoadLive.svelte' with { wake: 'load' };
  import IdleLive from './IdleLive.svelte' with { wake: 'idle' };
  import VisibleLive from './VisibleLive.svelte' with { wake: 'visible' };
</script>

<div class="doc-demo-row">
  <figure class="doc-demo">
    <figcaption><code>wake: 'load'</code></figcaption>
    <LoadLive />
  </figure>
  <figure class="doc-demo">
    <figcaption><code>wake: 'idle'</code></figcaption>
    <IdleLive />
  </figure>
  <figure class="doc-demo">
    <figcaption><code>wake: 'visible'</code></figcaption>
    <VisibleLive />
  </figure>
</div>

The `Live since …` / `Idle after …` / `In view …` stamps are written by each island's `$effect` after it wakes, so they prove the component actually hydrated rather than shipping a baked value — and the `idle` and `visible` ones wake later than `load`. The full schedule reference, plus `visible` margin tuning, lives in [Timing & nesting](/docs/islands/timing-nesting).

## Props cross the boundary

Props are real Svelte props into the wrapper, serialized with devalue for the island payload. So they must be serializable — numbers, strings, plain objects, arrays. What cannot cross:

- **Functions** (an `onSave` callback) — devalue can't carry a closure. Do the work inside the island, or call a [remote function](/docs/data-state/remote-functions).
- **Host children / snippets** — put the island's UI and its [lakes](/docs/islands/lakes) inside the island's own `.svelte` file.
- **Live class instances** — unless the class opts in as [shared state](/docs/data-state/state-context#sharing-state).

See [Constraints](/docs/reference/constraints) for the full list.

## A worked example: a cart badge

A header badge that reflects a cart, hydrated on `load` so it is live immediately:

```svelte
<!-- CartBadge.svelte -->
<script>
  let { count = 0 } = $props();
</script>

<span class="badge">{count}</span>
```

```svelte
<!-- +page.svelte -->
<script>
  import CartBadge from '$lib/CartBadge.svelte' with { wake: 'load' };
</script>

<CartBadge count={items.length} />
```

To let a *separate* "Add to cart" island update this badge, the two share one live object — see [Shared state](/docs/data-state/state-context#sharing-state).

## Do / don't

- **Do** default anything below the fold to `wake: 'visible'` — it is free performance.
- **Do** keep islands small and focused; the smaller the island, the less JS ships.
- **Don't** pass callbacks or class instances as props expecting them to work — they can't cross the wire. Move the logic inside, or reach for a remote function or shared state.
- **Don't** wrap the whole page in one island — that is just `csr = true` with extra steps.


